14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L20",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 16–17, 1862","place":"The northern Antietam battlefield between the Upper Bridge and Pry Ford, the Miller farm, Smoketown Road, and the East Woods north of Sharpsburg, Maryland","result":"Joseph Hooker’s I Corps crossed Antietam Creek on September 16 and advanced against Robert E. Lee’s left. George G. Meade’s Pennsylvania Reserves fought John B. Hood’s Confederates around the East Woods until darkness. The United States troops retained an advanced foothold, Joseph K. F. Mansfield’s XII Corps crossed near midnight to support them, and both sides prepared to renew the battle at dawn","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The opening infantry fight at Antietam on Tuesday evening, September 16","note":"The episode backtracks from the planned dawn attack to examine Hooker’s crossing, the East Woods skirmish, and the wet night before the main battle."},{"label":"Hooker’s orders","value":"Cross the Antietam, incline left, and feel for Lee’s left flank","note":"Hooker received the order between 1 and 2 p.m. His official report says he was to attack the Confederate left; the episode emphasizes that McClellan supplied few additional details."},{"label":"I Corps strength","value":"About 12,000–13,000 in Hooker’s official report","note":"Hooker told McClellan that his corps had already lost nearly 1,000 men at South Mountain and asked for prompt reinforcement."},{"label":"Crossing begins","value":"Hooker’s three divisions started across at about 4 p.m.","note":"The episode’s time is consistent with the Army staff-ride chronology, which places the crossing at approximately 3:30–4 p.m."},{"label":"Upper Bridge","value":"George G. Meade’s and James B. Ricketts’s divisions crossed here","note":"Meade’s Pennsylvania Reserves led the corps and then turned across country toward the Confederate left."},{"label":"Pry Ford","value":"Abner Doubleday’s division crossed just downstream","note":"The ford had been located and improved during the approach to Sharpsburg. It is distinct from the Pry House, McClellan’s battlefield headquarters farther south."},{"label":"Hooker’s approach","value":"Gain the divide, then incline south toward the Confederate flank","note":"Meade advanced first. Hooker’s official report describes the initial direction as nearly perpendicular to Antietam Creek before the corps turned left."},{"label":"Northern battlefield triangle","value":"Hagerstown Pike, Smoketown Road, and Dunker Church","note":"The roads met near the church. The Miller cornfield occupied much of the ground between them, with the West Woods on the pike side and the East Woods along the Federal approach."},{"label":"Miller’s cornfield","value":"About 30 acres in the episode’s description","note":"The field extended east from the Hagerstown Pike toward the East Woods and became the principal landmark of the next morning’s fighting."},{"label":"Confederate warning","value":"J. E. B. Stuart’s mounted sentries detected Hooker’s movement","note":"The episode traces the report from vedettes near the Upper Bridge to Stuart at Dunker Church, then to Lee, Longstreet, and Jackson in Sharpsburg."},{"label":"Lee’s response","value":"John B. Hood’s division moved north to protect the army’s left","note":"Lee directed Longstreet to send Hood. Once in the northern sector, Hood operated in Stonewall Jackson’s area of responsibility."},{"label":"Hood’s command arrangement","value":"A Longstreet division temporarily taking orders from Jackson","note":"Hood’s two brigades were commanded by Col. William T. Wofford and Col. Evander M. Law. The episode uses the transfer to illustrate Lee’s flexible battlefield command structure."},{"label":"Hood’s initial line","value":"From Hagerstown Pike along the south edge of the cornfield into the East Woods","note":"The exhausted division had been resting near Dunker Church before the long roll called it into line."},{"label":"Condition of Hood’s men","value":"Fatigued, hungry, and living largely on corn and green apples","note":"They had marched hard through the Maryland Campaign and had not received a proper meal. The episode connects the poor diet with widespread illness in the ranks."},{"label":"Long-range Federal artillery","value":"Heavy guns fired from the high ground east of Antietam Creek","note":"The episode identifies 20-pounder Parrott rifles in the Army of the Potomac’s artillery reserve near the Pry House. Their range allowed them to reach Hood’s movement north of Dunker Church."},{"label":"Confederate skirmish screen","value":"About 100 men of the 4th Texas occupied a fence-line position","note":"Hood sent skirmishers ahead of the main division. The episode places this detachment beyond the East Woods with a clear field of fire toward the approaching Federals."},{"label":"Federal advance guard","value":"The 13th Pennsylvania Reserves, the Bucktails","note":"Hooker personally selected Col. Hugh W. McNeil’s regiment as skirmishers. Four companies led on both sides of Smoketown Road while six followed in support."},{"label":"Bucktail equipment","value":"Sharps breech-loading rifles","note":"The rifles could be loaded while the men lay prone, letting the regiment keep firing from shallow furrows as it crossed exposed ground."},{"label":"Contact","value":"The 4th Texas opened fire at about 6 p.m.","note":"After roughly fifteen minutes of exchanging fire, McNeil ordered the Bucktails forward across the plowed field toward the fence."},{"label":"McNeil’s death","value":"Killed while leading the final rush","note":"The episode says McNeil was shot in the chest as he called the Bucktails forward. His men carried the fence and pursued the Confederate skirmishers into the East Woods."},{"label":"Bucktail losses in the episode","value":"29 killed and 65 wounded during the evening advance","note":"The episode attributes these figures to the movement across the open ground. Regimental and army returns often combine casualties from September 16 and 17, so totals vary by accounting method."},{"label":"Confederate counter","value":"5th Texas and 18th Georgia checked the Bucktails in the woods","note":"Capt. Ike Turner led the 5th Texas forward as the 4th Texas fell back; the 18th Georgia advanced on its left."},{"label":"Seymour reinforces","value":"The rest of First Brigade secured a Federal foothold","note":"Truman Seymour sent the 1st, 2nd, 5th, and 6th Pennsylvania Reserves forward after the Bucktails disappeared into the trees."},{"label":"Meade deploys the division","value":"Magilton’s and Anderson’s brigades moved up in support","note":"Meade’s remaining brigades formed and advanced while Capt. Dunbar R. Ransom’s Battery C, 5th U.S. Artillery, enfiladed Confederate infantry and artillery near the cornfield."},{"label":"End of the evening fight","value":"Darkness stopped the organized musketry and cannon fire","note":"The United States held part of the East Woods. Hood withdrew toward the West Woods, while the opposing pickets remained close enough to exchange fire through the damp night."},{"label":"XII Corps reinforcement","value":"Mansfield crossed shortly before midnight","note":"The corps moved into a supporting bivouac north and rear of Hooker. The episode places the end of its confused, rain-soaked night march at about 2 a.m."},{"label":"Casualty reporting limit","value":"No complete separate total survives for the September 16 clash","note":"The episode explains that many Confederate reports merged losses from the evening and the next day. The guide therefore does not present a combined battlefield total for this preliminary action."},{"label":"Contested interpretation","value":"Did Hooker’s movement sacrifice surprise or draw troops north?","note":"Longstreet and Stephen W. Sears argued that the crossing revealed the Federal approach. The hosts contend that it fit McClellan’s plan to pressure both flanks before striking Lee’s center. That defense is the episode’s interpretation, not a settled fact."},{"label":"Important Confederate loss","value":"Col. Phillip F. Liddell, 11th Mississippi, mortally wounded","note":"The transcript renders his name as “Philip Little.” Historical records identify Col. Phillip Frank Liddell, wounded on September 16 and dead at Shepherdstown on September 25."},{"label":"Recommended field guide","value":"“Antietam Expedition Guide” by TravelBrains and the History Channel","note":"The hosts recommend the self-guided audio tour and accompanying booklet for a battlefield visit."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Liddell, Magilton, Pry Ford, Hagerstown Pike, Smoketown Road, Dunker Church, Sharps rifle, Parrott rifle, and Poffenberger","note":"The automated transcript produces phonetic or variant forms including “Philip Little,” “Magelton,” “Sharp’s,” and “Parrot.” This guide uses standard historical spellings."}],"maps":[{"title":"Hooker crosses and turns toward Lee’s left","caption":"Meade and Ricketts crossed the Upper Bridge while Doubleday used Pry Ford. Hooker first moved toward the divide west of Antietam Creek, then inclined south behind Meade. Stuart’s sentries detected the crossing, and Lee sent Hood north toward the cornfield and East Woods. Routes and positions are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History staff-ride guide, Hooker’s and Meade’s official reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep190-hooker-crossing.svg","alt":"Schematic map showing Meade and Ricketts crossing the Upper Bridge, Doubleday crossing Pry Ford, Hooker turning south toward the East Woods, and Hood moving north from Dunker Church."},{"title":"The evening fight at the East Woods","caption":"The Bucktails advanced toward 4th Texas skirmishers at a fence line, fought forward by short rushes, and lost Col. Hugh W. McNeil during the final advance. The 5th Texas and 18th Georgia countered, Seymour brought up the rest of his brigade, and Federal artillery enfiladed Confederate positions near the cornfield. Exact fence and unit footprints are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Meade’s official report, Antietam National Battlefield East Woods interpretation, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep190-east-woods-evening.svg","alt":"Schematic action map of the Bucktails advancing on Fourth Texas skirmishers, McNeil falling near the fence, Confederate reinforcements countering through the East Woods, and Seymour’s brigade securing a Federal foothold."},{"title":"The Miller farm after the battle","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed Union soldiers looking across the Miller farm toward the West Woods on September 19. Fallen Confederates lie in the foreground. The view records the ground beside the cornfield and woods that shaped the fighting, although it was made three days after the evening action in this episode.","source":"Library of Congress, Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep190-miller-farm-west-woods.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of Union soldiers and fallen Confederate soldiers on the Miller farm, looking toward the West Woods after Antietam."},{"title":"Hooker’s Antietam headquarters","caption":"Alexander Gardner’s September 1862 photograph identifies this house as Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam. It provides a contemporary view connected to the I Corps commander whose advance opened the battle.","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep190-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner glass-negative view of the house identified as Joseph Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"The episode returns to Tuesday evening","detail":"Hooker’s I Corps has crossed Antietam Creek and is moving toward the northern end of Lee’s line."},{"time":"1:34","audioTime":94,"title":"McClellan’s order is summarized","detail":"Hooker is told to cross, incline left, move south toward Sharpsburg, and feel for the Confederate flank."},{"time":"1:58","audioTime":118,"title":"I Corps starts across","detail":"The episode places the beginning of the three-division crossing at about 4 p.m."},{"time":"2:10","audioTime":130,"title":"The crossings divide by route","detail":"Meade and Ricketts use the Upper Bridge while Doubleday crosses downstream at Pry Ford."},{"time":"2:20","audioTime":140,"title":"Hooker seeks clarification","detail":"He rides to McClellan’s headquarters, receives no more precise direction, and is promised command of any reinforcements sent to him."},{"time":"2:56","audioTime":176,"title":"Meade leads the probe","detail":"The Pennsylvania Reserves advance toward the East Woods at the head of I Corps."},{"time":"3:13","audioTime":193,"title":"The road triangle is drawn","detail":"Hagerstown Pike and Smoketown Road meet near Dunker Church, framing the northern battlefield."},{"time":"4:19","audioTime":259,"title":"The cornfield fills the center","detail":"The episode locates David R. Miller’s field between the roads and the two woodlots."},{"time":"5:11","audioTime":311,"title":"Stuart’s sentries spot Hooker","detail":"Mounted vedettes near the Upper Bridge report the Federal movement through Stuart to Lee."},{"time":"5:47","audioTime":347,"title":"Lee sends Hood north","detail":"Hood’s division is shifted from Longstreet’s command area to meet the threat on Jackson’s front."},{"time":"6:32","audioTime":392,"title":"Hood’s two brigades form","detail":"Wofford’s and Law’s brigades move beyond Dunker Church into line along the cornfield and East Woods."},{"time":"7:24","audioTime":444,"title":"Exhaustion and hunger are described","detail":"Hood’s men have marched hard since Hagerstown and subsisted largely on corn and green apples."},{"time":"8:33","audioTime":513,"title":"Heavy Federal guns open","detail":"Long-range artillery from the high ground near the Pry House shells Hood’s movement."},{"time":"10:32","audioTime":632,"title":"The 4th Texas takes the skirmish line","detail":"About 100 Texans move to a fence-line position ahead of Hood’s main force."},{"time":"10:59","audioTime":659,"title":"Hooker selects the Bucktails","detail":"The 13th Pennsylvania Reserves advances toward the East Woods as Meade’s skirmish screen."},{"time":"12:37","audioTime":757,"title":"Col. Hugh W. McNeil is introduced","detail":"The episode explains his experience, the regiment’s bucktail badge, and its reputation for marksmanship."},{"time":"13:21","audioTime":801,"title":"Breech-loading rifles matter","detail":"The Bucktails carry Sharps rifles, which soon let them reload while prone in the furrows."},{"time":"14:10","audioTime":850,"title":"The skirmish formation advances","detail":"Four Bucktail companies lead on both sides of Smoketown Road while six companies follow in reserve."},{"time":"14:52","audioTime":892,"title":"The first infantry fire begins","detail":"At about 6 p.m., the waiting 4th Texas skirmishers fire and the Bucktails answer."},{"time":"15:30","audioTime":930,"title":"McNeil orders an advance","detail":"The Pennsylvanians move across the exposed plowed field after roughly fifteen minutes of skirmishing."},{"time":"15:49","audioTime":949,"title":"The Bucktails go to ground","detail":"Intense fire stops a conventional advance about 75 yards from the fence, so the men fire prone and move in short rushes."},{"time":"16:39","audioTime":999,"title":"McNeil leads the charge and falls","detail":"He calls the Bucktails forward, is shot in the chest, and dies as the regiment surges toward the fence."},{"time":"16:54","audioTime":1014,"title":"The Bucktails take the fence","detail":"The regiment drives back the Confederate skirmishers and continues into the East Woods."},{"time":"17:19","audioTime":1039,"title":"The Confederate counter begins","detail":"Ike Turner’s 5th Texas and the 18th Georgia help stop and push back the Bucktails in the fading light."},{"time":"18:11","audioTime":1091,"title":"Seymour sends in First Brigade","detail":"The 1st, 2nd, 5th, and 6th Pennsylvania Reserves advance to support the Bucktails."},{"time":"18:47","audioTime":1127,"title":"The Federal foothold holds","detail":"Seymour’s brigade becomes closely engaged and retains an advanced position in the East Woods."},{"time":"19:14","audioTime":1154,"title":"Ransom’s battery enfilades the Confederates","detail":"Battery C, 5th U.S. Artillery, fires from the woods’ edge into Confederate infantry and guns near the cornfield, forcing the artillery to shift."},{"time":"19:49","audioTime":1189,"title":"Darkness ends the organized fight","detail":"Musketry and cannon fire subside after the United States advance has secured ground west of the creek."},{"time":"20:21","audioTime":1221,"title":"Hooker asks for support","detail":"Concerned about his isolated corps, Hooker tells McClellan that prompt reinforcements are essential."},{"time":"20:46","audioTime":1246,"title":"Mansfield is ordered across","detail":"McClellan sends XII Corps to support Hooker on the west side of Antietam Creek."},{"time":"21:16","audioTime":1276,"title":"XII Corps struggles through the rain","detail":"New regiments stumble through the dark and finally halt in bivouac at about 2 a.m."},{"time":"21:59","audioTime":1319,"title":"Hooker promises a dawn attack","detail":"He reports that the assault will resume when there is enough light to distinguish friend from foe."},{"time":"22:26","audioTime":1346,"title":"The casualty record remains incomplete","detail":"The episode explains why many Confederate reports combine losses from Tuesday evening and Wednesday."},{"time":"22:58","audioTime":1378,"title":"Longstreet judges the preliminary fight","detail":"He later argues that the Federal gain was limited and that Hooker’s advance disclosed McClellan’s purpose."},{"time":"23:40","audioTime":1420,"title":"Sears criticizes the loss of surprise","detail":"The episode presents Stephen W. Sears’s argument that Lee used the warning to strengthen his left."},{"time":"24:22","audioTime":1462,"title":"The hosts challenge that interpretation","detail":"They argue that Hooker’s movement drew Confederate strength north as McClellan’s broader plan intended."},{"time":"26:19","audioTime":1579,"title":"The episode explains its backtrack","detail":"The hosts decided that the September 16 action deserved fuller treatment before the main battle began."},{"time":"27:11","audioTime":1631,"title":"McNeil and Liddell define the stakes","detail":"The deaths of the Bucktail colonel and the 11th Mississippi’s mortally wounded commander show that the preliminary fight was not minor to its participants."},{"time":"27:22","audioTime":1642,"title":"The field-guide recommendation closes the episode","detail":"The hosts recommend the Antietam Expedition Guide for its audio tour and battlefield booklet."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Ordered I Corps across Antietam Creek against Lee’s left and sent XII Corps to reinforce Hooker during the night"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps","subordinates":"Directed the crossing and southward advance, asked for reinforcement, and reported that he would renew the attack at dawn"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday","command":"First Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed at Pry Ford and advanced on the right of the other I Corps divisions"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts","command":"Second Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed at the Upper Bridge behind or beside Meade’s leading division"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George G. Meade","command":"Third Division, I Corps · Pennsylvania Reserves","subordinates":"Led Hooker’s advance, deployed skirmishers toward the East Woods, and brought artillery and the remainder of the division forward"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Truman Seymour","command":"First Brigade, Meade’s Division","subordinates":"Supported the Bucktails with the 1st, 2nd, 5th, and 6th Pennsylvania Reserves and held the advanced East Woods position"},{"commander":"Col. Hugh W. McNeil","command":"13th Pennsylvania Reserves · First Pennsylvania Rifles · “Bucktails”","subordinates":"Led the Federal skirmish line toward the fence and was killed while ordering the final advance"},{"commander":"Col. Albert L. Magilton","command":"Second Brigade, Meade’s Division","subordinates":"Formed and advanced in support of Seymour as the fight expanded around the East Woods"},{"commander":"Lt. Col. Robert Anderson","command":"Third Brigade, Meade’s Division","subordinates":"Formed and advanced with Magilton’s brigade in support of the engaged Federal line"},{"commander":"Capt. Dunbar R. Ransom","command":"Battery C, 5th U.S. Artillery","subordinates":"Opened an enfilading fire from the woods’ edge that drove the opposing battery to another position before darkness"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","command":"XII Corps","subordinates":"Crossed Antietam Creek near midnight and bivouacked north and rear of I Corps before the dawn battle"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Reacted to Stuart’s warning by directing that Hood’s division move north to protect the army’s left"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Confederate left wing","subordinates":"Held responsibility for the northern battlefield and took operational control of Hood’s division after it shifted north"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Confederate right wing","subordinates":"Received Lee’s direction to detach Hood from his normal command and send the division toward Jackson’s sector"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Used mounted sentries near the Upper Bridge to watch the Confederate left and passed word of Hooker’s crossing to Lee"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John B. Hood","command":"Hood’s Division","subordinates":"Moved north from near Dunker Church, placed Wofford on the left and Law on the right, and sent skirmishers toward the Federal approach"},{"commander":"Col. William T. Wofford","command":"Texas Brigade, Hood’s Division","subordinates":"Held the division left along Hagerstown Pike and the cornfield; the 4th Texas furnished the principal skirmish detachment"},{"commander":"Col. Evander M. Law","command":"Law’s Brigade, Hood’s Division","subordinates":"Held the division right toward the East Woods; the 11th Mississippi lost Col. Phillip F. Liddell in the evening action"},{"commander":"Capt. Isaac N. M. “Ike” Turner","command":"5th Texas Infantry","subordinates":"Led the regiment into the East Woods to support the withdrawing 4th Texas skirmishers and check the Bucktails"},{"commander":"Col. Phillip F. Liddell","command":"11th Mississippi Infantry, Law’s Brigade","subordinates":"Mortally wounded while commanding his regiment during the September 16 defense south of the East Woods"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who ordered Hooker across the creek and Mansfield to reinforce him","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"I Corps commander who directed the Federal crossing and opening advance against Lee’s left","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep190-hooker-headquarters.jpg","source":"Library of Congress"},{"name":"George G. Meade","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves division commander whose troops led I Corps and fought for the East Woods","side":"United States"},{"name":"Abner Doubleday","role":"I Corps division commander whose troops crossed at Pry Ford","side":"United States"},{"name":"James B. Ricketts","role":"I Corps division commander who crossed at the Upper Bridge","side":"United States"},{"name":"Truman Seymour","role":"Federal brigade commander who reinforced the Bucktails and secured the East Woods foothold","side":"United States"},{"name":"Hugh W. McNeil","role":"Colonel of the Bucktails killed while leading the advance on the Confederate skirmish line","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield","role":"XII Corps commander ordered across Antietam Creek during the night to support Hooker","side":"United States"},{"name":"Dunbar R. Ransom","role":"Regular Army battery commander whose light 12-pounders enfiladed Confederate infantry and artillery near the East Woods","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who shifted Hood north after receiving Stuart’s warning","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Commander of the Confederate left who gained operational control of Hood’s division in the northern sector","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander who sent Hood’s division north on Lee’s order","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Cavalry commander whose vedettes discovered Hooker’s movement and relayed the warning","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John B. Hood","role":"Division commander who opposed Meade around the cornfield and East Woods","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William T. Wofford","role":"Commander of Hood’s Texas Brigade on the Confederate left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Evander M. Law","role":"Commander of Hood’s second brigade on the Confederate right toward the East Woods","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Isaac N. M. “Ike” Turner","role":"Captain who led the 5th Texas counter into the East Woods","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Phillip F. Liddell","role":"Colonel of the 11th Mississippi mortally wounded in the September 16 action","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George W. Smalley","role":"New-York Tribune correspondent whose account described the evening fight fading in the darkness","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Col. Hugh W. McNeil","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 13th Pennsylvania Reserves (First Pennsylvania Rifles or Bucktails)","outcome":"Shot in the chest and killed while calling the regiment forward near the East Woods on September 16; the Bucktails carried the contested fence line after he fell"},{"name":"Col. Phillip F. Liddell","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander, 11th Mississippi Infantry, Law’s Brigade, Hood’s Division","outcome":"Mortally wounded while defending the Confederate line south of the East Woods on September 16; died at Shepherdstown on September 25, 1862"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep190-hooker-crossing.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Hooker’s I Corps crossing Antietam Creek and turning south toward Lee’s left.","caption":"Meade and Ricketts crossed at the Upper Bridge, Doubleday used Pry Ford, and the corps turned south. Stuart’s warning brought Hood north to meet the movement.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, Official Records, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep190-east-woods-evening.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the September 16 evening action at the East Woods.","caption":"The Bucktails led the Federal approach against 4th Texas skirmishers. Confederate reinforcements checked their advance, while Seymour’s brigade and Federal artillery preserved a United States foothold as darkness fell.","source":"Meade’s official report, Antietam National Battlefield, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep190-miller-farm-west-woods.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of Union soldiers and Confederate dead on the Miller farm after Antietam.","caption":"Gardner’s September 19 view looks from the Miller farm toward the West Woods. It documents the terrain around the cornfield and woodlots after the battle; the fallen men make the image graphic.","source":"Library of Congress, Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs; no known restrictions on publication."},{"image":"/images/ep190-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the building identified as Joseph Hooker’s Antietam headquarters.","caption":"This September 1862 glass-negative view records the headquarters associated with Hooker, the I Corps commander at the center of the episode.","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints; no known restrictions on publication."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 190 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/04/09/civil-war-podcast-episode-190/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The East Woods","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-east-woods.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Guide to the Battle of Antietam","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/35-3-1.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Maryland and Fredericksburg Campaigns, 1862–1863","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-6.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I: Maryland Campaign reports","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: George B. McClellan’s official campaign reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=19"},{"label":"Primary source: Joseph Hooker’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=246"},{"label":"Primary source: George G. Meade’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=25"},{"label":"Primary source: Abner Doubleday’s official reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=47"},{"label":"Primary source: John B. Hood’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=31"},{"label":"Primary source: James Longstreet’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=26"},{"label":"Primary source: William T. Wofford’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=99"},{"label":"Primary source: Dunbar R. Ransom’s battery report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=442"},{"label":"Antietam on the Web: Col. Hugh Watson McNeil","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/officers.php?officer_id=239"},{"label":"Antietam on the Web: Col. Phillip Frank Liddell","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/officers.php?officer_id=535"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Atlas of the battlefield of Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Gardner view on the Miller farm","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646937/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Joseph Hooker’s Antietam headquarters","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671472/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","The Miller farm after the battle",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep190-miller-farm-west-woods.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open The Miller farm after the battle at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep190-miller-farm-west-woods.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of Union soldiers and fallen Confederate soldiers on the Miller farm, looking toward the West Woods after Antietam.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The Miller farm after the battle"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alexander Gardner photographed Union soldiers looking across the Miller farm toward the West Woods on September 19. Fallen Confederates lie in the foreground. The view records the ground beside the cornfield and woods that shaped the fighting, although it was made three days after the evening action in this episode."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep190-miller-farm-west-woods.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress, Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Hooker’s Antietam headquarters",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$"